<ol>
<li id="fn:1">
<p>The &ldquo;damaged&rdquo; message is misleading because it&rsquo;s a fraction of a potential truth. Apple requires developers to pay a yearly fee to get a license. The other part Apple does is a scan for malicious software (malware), which happens on Apple&rsquo;s servers. But to upload to Apple&rsquo;s servers, the developer needs the license first. On macOS, <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-euro/guide/security/sec5599b66df/web">Gatekeeper</a> checks for a license and for a notarization ticket — the approval from Apple&rsquo;s servers that the software is &ldquo;free of known malicious content, and hasn&rsquo;t been altered.&rdquo; In the case of LibreWolf, because the developers refused to join Apple&rsquo;s paid developer program, LibreWolf never got the chance to be uploaded to Apple for a scan — it was rejected outright. To call such software damaged then is a big stretch. This is exactly the kind of <em>lack</em> of transparency I dislike about Apple.&#160;<a href="#fnref:1" class="footnote-backref" role="doc-backlink">&#x21a9;&#xfe0e;</a></p>
</li>
</ol>
